                    r4_resolve_label:       `How firmly do you hold that durable authority beats a one-day performance of confidence? (%)`,
                    proc_surface:           `Surface the skeptic's case, use the veteran's knowledge, then make and own the call`,
                    proc_overquestion:      `Keep opening it — run more discussions; it's Thursday and there's still no decision`,
                    proc_impose:            `Impose your preferred architecture and shut down the debate to look decisive`,
                    dir_honest:             `Honest — here is what I learned from the squad, here is my call, and here is what I still don't know`,
                    dir_confidence:         `Project total certainty — never admit a single thing you're unsure about`,
                    dir_please:             `Quietly switch to the option you think the director secretly prefers`,
                    contract_explicit:      `Yes — name exactly when you'll open the floor and when you'll decide, in writing to the squad`,
                    contract_implicit:      `No need — the good freeze call speaks for itself; they'll figure out the rest`,
                    contract_control:       `Reserve all decisions to yourself from now on — you've earned the right to lead`,

                    warn_humility_range:    `Your reflective-honesty level must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_safety_range:      `The psychological-safety level must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_role_range:        `The substance of the veteran's role must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_resolve_range:     `Your resolve must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_earned:        `<strong>Right diagnosis.</strong> Authority here is earned through expertise and the squad's consent — the title bought you nothing but a chair. Now you can work to earn the room instead of demanding it.`,
                    coach_r1_title:         `<strong>“The call is mine because I was promoted.”</strong> That's the one belief this culture punishes. Eleven engineers don't grant authority to a badge — they grant it to reasoning and credibility. You've already started spending capital you don't have.`,
                    coach_r1_director:      `<strong>The director's backing is not your authority.</strong> His support gets you the title; only the squad can grant the authority. Leaning on his expectation to “take charge” is exactly the over-assert reflex the culture rejects.`,
                    coach_r1_seniority:     `<strong>Being the best engineer got you the job, not the authority.</strong> Technical merit is necessary but not sufficient here — the squad still has to choose to follow you. Don't confuse respect for your code with consent to your leadership.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp read.</strong> You named authority as earned AND focused on what each member needs to grant consent. That's the Learning-Teams move — learn the team before you lead it, and you'll design a first week that actually lands.`,
                    coach_r1_plan:          `<strong>A decisive week-one plan is the trap.</strong> Performing decisiveness before you understand the squad reads as the over-assert reflex. Slow down: what does each of these eleven people actually need from you?`,
                    coach_r1_arch:          `<strong>“The people side will sort itself out” — it won't.</strong> The Friday freeze is hostage to the veteran's knowledge and the skeptic's buy-in. The relationships are the work, not a distraction from it.`,
                    coach_r1_allies:        `<strong>Sorting the room into loyalists and threats?</strong> That's how you guarantee the 5th failed promotion. The skeptic and the veteran aren't enemies to manage out — they're the people whose consent you most need to earn.`,
                    coach_r1_nohumility:    `<strong>You've admitted nothing you don't know.</strong> In a Learning-Teams culture, that's not strength — it's the false-certainty trap. The squad reads false confidence instantly. What would it cost you to say out loud what you're still figuring out?`,
                    coach_r2_merits:        `<strong>You answered on the merits and invited the team in.</strong> That's how authority is built in an expert culture — you won a point of credibility and opened the squad's voice in the same sixty seconds.`,
                    coach_r2_rank:          `<strong>“Because I'm the lead now.”</strong> In this company that's the one sentence that loses the room — eleven engineers just watched you reach for the badge instead of the argument. The skeptic now has proof you can't win on reasoning. Your credibility account just took a hard hit.`,
                    coach_r2_defer:         `<strong>You opened everything and committed to nothing.</strong> That's not humility, it's a vacuum — and your team can feel it. Followers grant authority to a leader who will <em>decide</em>, not one who hides behind consensus. When does inquiry end and your call begin?`,
                    coach_r2_dodge:         `<strong>You changed the subject.</strong> The skeptic asked a fair question in front of the room and you ducked it — so the room now believes you don't have an answer. A dodged challenge is a lost one.`,
                    coach_r2_calibrated:    `<strong>This is the calibration the case is about.</strong> Answering on the merits AND committing to question-then-decide tells the squad you'll listen <em>and</em> lead. That's the stance that earns durable authority.`,
                    coach_r2_directive:     `<strong>Setting direction firmly and expecting them to follow</strong> reads as command in a culture that runs on debate. You may get compliance; you won't get the consent that makes authority stick. Reason in the open instead.`,
                    coach_r2_facilitative:  `<strong>Keeping every decision open isn't safe — it's a vacuum.</strong> By Friday you owe the committee an architecture call. Followers grant authority to someone who will close the question, not someone who never does.`,
                    coach_r2_nosafety:      `<strong>You signalled that dissent isn't welcome.</strong> In your first week that produces compliant silence, not safety — and silence kills the decision quality you need for the freeze. The skeptic's challenge was your chance to open voice, not close it.`,
                    coach_r3_consent:       `<strong>You read it right: this is withheld consent, not low output.</strong> She's deciding whether to follow you. Name that honestly and you can earn her back — and her legacy knowledge with her.`,
                    coach_r3_performance:   `<strong>You're planning to write her up for “low output.”</strong> But she's not failing — she's withholding consent and the legacy knowledge your Friday freeze depends on, because she was passed over and no one acknowledged it. Discipline her and you confirm the squad's worst fear about you.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Routing around the veteran doesn't solve the authority problem — it advertises it.</strong> The squad watches whether you can win back the person you beat. Use someone else's knowledge and the legacy gaps surface on Thursday night.`,
                    coach_r3_acknowledge:   `<strong>That's the conversation that earns her back.</strong> Acknowledging the passed-over disappointment and defining a real role for her expertise treats her as the swing vote she is — follower engagement is the substrate of your authority.`,
                    coach_r3_overpromise:   `<strong>Hinting she'll be the next lead is a debt you can't pay.</strong> Over-promising buys a week of cooperation and a year of betrayal. Trust built on a promise you don't control collapses the moment it's tested.`,
                    coach_r3_patronise:     `<strong>“Still valued” without naming the elephant lands as patronising.</strong> She knows she was passed over; pretending otherwise insults her. Name it honestly or she'll read your reassurance as exactly the management-speak she's tuning out.`,
                    coach_r3_norole:        `<strong>A vague role is no role.</strong> If you don't define something real for her legacy-codebase expertise, “we value you” is just words — and the Friday freeze stays hostage to a relationship you didn't repair.`,
                    coach_r4_surface:       `<strong>You ran it the way the culture demands.</strong> Surfacing the skeptic's case, using the veteran's knowledge, then making and owning the call — that's authority in an expert culture: open the reasoning, then close the question.`,
                    coach_r4_overquestion:  `<strong>It's Thursday and there's still no decision.</strong> Everyone feels heard and the committee meets tomorrow with nothing to freeze. Inquiry without a decision point is just delay. Authority includes the moment you close the question and own the call.`,
                    coach_r4_impose:        `<strong>You shut down the debate to look decisive.</strong> In a debate-driven culture that's pulling rank with extra steps — you'll get the freeze and lose the room. Decisiveness isn't silencing dissent; it's deciding <em>after</em> you've surfaced it.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's the durable answer.</strong> Showing what you learned, owning your call, and naming what you still don't know is exactly the reflective honesty this culture rewards over performed certainty — and the director saw real authority, not a show.`,
                    coach_r4_confidence:    `<strong>You projected total certainty.</strong> It plays for a day, but your culture's radar for false confidence is exact and your skeptic is sharper than your script. Admitting one real unknown would have built more authority than pretending you have none.`,
                    coach_r4_please:        `<strong>You quietly switched to the director's preference.</strong> The squad sees it instantly — you traded the squad's consent for the boss's approval, and forfeited both your credibility and the decision quality you spent the week building.`,
                    coach_r4_contract:      `<strong>You made the implicit explicit.</strong> Naming when you'll open the floor and when you'll decide is what stabilises authority and prevents the velocity collapse your predecessors suffered. Good call, plus a contract the squad can hold you to.`,
                    coach_r4_implicit:      `<strong>Good call on the architecture — but no operating agreement.</strong> Your squad still doesn't know how the next hundred decisions get made. The velocity drop your predecessors suffered came from exactly that ambiguity.`,
                    coach_r4_control:       `<strong>Reserving every decision to yourself undoes the whole week.</strong> You'd be telling a debate-driven squad that their voice ends now — the surest path to the velocity collapse and the 5th failed promotion. Authority you hoard is authority you lose.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Diagnosed earned authority; read what the team needs.`,
